export const body = "<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>In this tutorial, Jessica Helgesson will show you how to build a modular system and some neat grid usage for level creation.</p>\n\n<p>Jessica used to work at EA DICE on <em>Battlefield 4</em>, <em>Star Wars Battlefront I</em> and its sequel.</p>\n\n<h2>Basics</h2>\n\n<p>Grid is a common feature in most 3D applications and game engines. Usually, you can control its size and choose whether to snap to it or not.</p>\n\n<p>Grid-based content generally has higher precision thanks to uniform corners. When you eyeball one asset to the next or vertex snap it, you might leave gaps or offset pieces, which can be problematic.</p>\n\n<p>When you place smaller assets, you'll want to snap to a grid with smaller squares; bigger assets thereby should snap to bigger squares.</p>\n\n<p>The pieces in this tutorial are rendered in Unreal where Z is up.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/dd5090b4c70f1c65fcdd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/dd5090b4c70f1c65fcdd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"866\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/e58ccbcf11e54317e959.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/e58ccbcf11e54317e959.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>In this image, the second example doesn’t have reference point on the world grid (grey) but has loads of reference points on its local grid (white).</p></div>\n\n<h2>Local vs world</h2>\n\n<p>Imperfections and organic placements are important to make a world feel believable. You can benefit from the grid and achieve believability at the same time by combining local grids with free placement in the world you make. Usually, this is done by using prefabs/blueprints where everything is built on the grid and then freely placed in your level (which makes it local).</p>\n\n<p>This approach allows you to freely modify your prefab/blueprint with snapping while keeping the unbound placement in the world. Another situation where you can use this method is placing lights in the prefabs instead of in the world.</p>\n\n<h2>Power of two</h2>\n\n<p>There are two common ways of working with the grid: power of two grid and unreal standard.</p>\n\n<p>In this tutorial, we will focus on the power of two (PoT) grid, which uses the same type of values for game textures; and the grid snapping levels are evenly distributed since it will always be two raised to the X power (essentially doubling the size at every level).</p>\n\n<p>The unreal standard uses more intuitive values because they are closer to real world measurements (5 m instead of 5.12 m, for example). Therefore, the snapping levels are quite irregular, and the texture sizes don’t align with the in-use values.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/c93896a01eff5b6a351e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/c93896a01eff5b6a351e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"865\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/f18b71621411215fd5e1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/f18b71621411215fd5e1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>If you were to place both the building and the pipes in the same level or blueprint, you would need to set the grid to 4096 to place the building and then bring it down to 64 to place the pipes.</p></div>\n\n<h2>Valuable values</h2>\n\n<p>When you create grid-based assets, you should stay close to the chosen values for that family of assets.</p>\n\n<p>For example, a <strong>pipe</strong> system benefits from a lower grid value within the <strong>32 – 128 range</strong>. And the <strong>1024 – 4096 range</strong> is better for <strong>buildings</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>The value you use for your system on should follow the boundary of your smallest piece. Therefore, it is better to have a pipe piece with a length of 64 cm than one with a length of 4096 cm (unless you are making an enormous gas pipeline).</p>\n\n<h2>Anatomy</h2>\n\n<p>This straight corridor piece is the most basic module in the set. It is built within a 512 x 512 x 512 boundary box (using 512 grid snapping). Keep in mind that the module doesn’t need to occupy all the space in the boundary box; it just needs to stay within that space and connects to the next module seamlessly.</p>\n\n<p>Note that floors, walls and ceilings need to have a thickness inside of the boundary box in order to avoid conflict between the Z axis and a potential neighboring piece.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/36d661a3f2af6d6e74c0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/36d661a3f2af6d6e74c0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>This specific set has a wall and floor thickness of 16 cm. The ceiling could potentially be a plane since it is very far from the border of the boundary box.</p></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/8c09bcd2a3fe98fc1edb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/8c09bcd2a3fe98fc1edb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Fail safe</h2>\n\n<p>To make sure that this piece on the 512 grid is working as intended, we simply duplicate and move it by grid snapping 512 in all directions. If they align perfectly without intruding others’ spaces, the module is working as intended.</p>\n\n<p>When two modules sit next to each another, the total wall thickness becomes 32 cm. Having another module on top of another module looks a bit unnatural, but we still have space within the boundary box below to iterate. Maybe we can add some supporting beams to make it more believable.</p>\n\n<h2>Pivot position</h2>\n\n<p>There are a few things to keep in mind when choosing the pivot position.</p>\n\n<p>To continue with 512 grid snapping, the pivot needs to be in the bottom corner of the object. That way, the pivot position, the piece’s size and the grid are all set to snap to 512. One drawback is that placing and rotating will need some extra clicks (irritating for large levels). On the other hand, you can put the pivot in the bottom middle with 256 grid snapping instead of 512, so you can rotate it in place.</p>\n\n<p>The pivot position not only affect grid snapping but also which way the object should be facing, which axis is up, and what applies to objects that are not built on the grid.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/9efdbe0033c624ddf58e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/9efdbe0033c624ddf58e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/384e51382f6467ff91dc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/384e51382f6467ff91dc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"862\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Base pieces</h2>\n\n<p>You can easily elaborate more pieces based on the original module. All variations are based on this one piece.</p>\n\n<p>It’d be wiser to keep the number of new pieces to the minimum while making them as reusable as possible. Reusable and flexible assets are more valuable than unique ones. Of course, custom memorable pieces are important too, but they come later.</p>\n\n<p>Similar to the original module, you should stress test your system, and make sure that all of your pieces align and that you can connect back to the first piece you placed using grid snapping (in this case, a 512 grid).</p>\n\n<h2>Add levels</h2>\n\n<p>The next level is 512 up because we work on a 512 grid. To smooth out the upward transition (avoid steepness), the stretch for this piece is 1536 cm (512 x 3). The beginning and end of this piece have a small, straight area to connect to regular straight pieces.</p>\n\n<p>All the pieces here are on the 512 grid in all directions. However, in production, keeping distances as short as possible (especially stairs) is better; it’s not uncommon to use a value of 384 (128 x 3) in height for a module. Lower height means more compact stair pieces which reduces the time the player spends walking. You just need to change the grid snapping to 128 from 512 when moving the piece up.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/eee15e1bda15056d45fd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/eee15e1bda15056d45fd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"865\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>We use a power of two value for each step (W: 32 cm, H: 16 cm) to get an even amount of them to the next level.</p></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/c436a3e8afe392e8f7eb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/c436a3e8afe392e8f7eb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>The choice is yours</h2>\n\n<p>The point of working on the grid is that you can create whatever you want within the reserved space. It can be a wall, a sci-fi corridor, an overpass, an organic cave with rock walls; the outside of the piece can be visible or not depending on which you prefer. This is simply a framework for your content to speak a mutual language and work together.</p>\n\n<p>The examples are different in style, but since they follow the same set of rules, they can coexist and even connect if we add a transition piece.</p>\n\n<h2>45 world or local</h2>\n\n<p>So far, we have added variations by introducing 45-degree modules; there are two ways to go about it.</p>\n\n<p>The first way is to continue to use world grid snapping. The pro is that your reference points to the world grid (control) are kept; the con is that you need more unique pieces.</p>\n\n<p>The second approach is to completely ignore the world grid and use the previously placed module as a reference point for the next one. The pro is that you can reuse 90-degree pieces and keep new pieces to a minimum. But you will have no smooth way to get back to the world grid.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a4b721104f449919dc75.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a4b721104f449919dc75.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/80691dc305f63a462e00.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/80691dc305f63a462e00.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>To create pieces with a 45-degree angle, you need to introduce at least 3 new pieces (or 2 where you need to mirror one if your engine allows it).</p></div>\n\n<h2>45-degree world</h2>\n\n<p>We are still using the corners as reference points, but the difference is that we use them diagonally instead of vertically or horizontally. This is why we cannot reuse the original straight module since the distance of the diagonal of a square is longer.</p>\n\n<p>If your modules have evenly-spaced details (pillars), you will need to adjust them slightly, but hopefully nothing impacts the overall look.</p>\n\n<p>Again, this method is preferred if you want to go from point A to point B and then connect back to point A but those require more pieces.</p>\n\n<h2>45-degree local</h2>\n\n<p>To create a 45-degree piece using the previously placed module, we only need one additional piece. Now, we will use the center of the object as the reference point instead of the grid corners.</p>\n\n<p>To place the next straight piece, we put it in the exact same spot as the one we just created, rotate it by 45 degrees, and then snap-move it 512 from that spot (use local translation, not world). The next piece needs to be placed in the exact same spot as the previous one, rotate and snap-moved again.</p>\n\n<p>This method allows for fewer pieces and tighter distances. It is ideal when you want to move from A to B without connecting back A again. Reconnecting back to A is hard because you lose the connection to the world grid after placing the first piece.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/ee07287f4da0a2fc5f81.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/ee07287f4da0a2fc5f81.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"862\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a31bdf6c95b52dc2fa72.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a31bdf6c95b52dc2fa72.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"864\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>22.5 world or local</h2>\n\n<p>You can add even more variations with the 22.5-degree angle. I haven't run across many systems using this in production, but it may come in handy sometimes.</p>\n\n<p>When you work on the world grid, it is important to know that you are not actually using the 22.5-degree angle. You are using 26.565- and 63.434-degree instead. This way of building introduces 6 new pieces.</p>\n\n<p>The second approach is similar to before. You can completely ignore the world grid and use the previous piece as reference with the benefit of only making 1 new piece.</p>\n\n<h2>How to 22.5 local</h2>\n\n<p>Here, we use the center of the object as the reference point instead of the corners of the grid. The way we place the 22.5-degree piece is very similar to that of the 45-degree piece.</p>\n\n<p>I places my piece at the exact same location as the previous piece, rotate it by 22.5-degree and snap-move it by 512.</p>\n\n<p>Similar to the 45-degree pieces, I only need to make one new piece to achieve this angle, but the cost is that I cannot connect back the world grid.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/3a7f4104c77698ceaaf5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/3a7f4104c77698ceaaf5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"864\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>To create a 22.5-degree piece using the previously placed module as reference, you only need 1 additional piece.</p></div>\n\n<h2>\"Fake\" power of two</h2>\n\n<p>You need to understand the distinction between power of two values and “fake” power of two values. A power of two value comes from 2<sup>x</sup>. 512 in most examples here is 2<sup>9</sup>. So when I create a set of modules, I start with determining the shortest one which then becomes the grid snapping I use.</p>\n\n<p>“Fake” is when you take a power of value and add it with another power of two value (256 +128 = 384) or add the same power of two values an amount of times that is not a power of two (256 + 256 + 256 = 768).</p>\n\n<p>Just note that they are two different systems using one approach each which makes it difficult to use together.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/82fc272fded1afb7fdf9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/82fc272fded1afb7fdf9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"867\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>Power of two values and “fake” power of two values don’t work similarly, but they are used together constantly. You opt for “fake” when you need tighter pieces that take up less space or you have tiling details or patterns better suited in those dimensions.</p>\n<p>The example pieces in this tutorial would probably get away with a height of 384 instead of 512, but I chose 512 for consistency.</p></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/1015599998ab3fdc778b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/1015599998ab3fdc778b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"864\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>In this example, I simply replace a boring straight area with a more organic module piece.</p></div>\n\n<h2>Custom</h2>\n\n<p>To extend the variety in your set, you will surely need custom pieces. However, they should only be created after you have ironed out your base set.</p>\n\n<p>The one rule to follow is that you need the custom pieces to begin and end on the grid with details that transition back to the original set.</p>\n\n<p>Custom can still be reused this way, but custom should mainly focus on introducing unique and memorable elements to your set instead.</p>\n\n<h2>Textures</h2>\n\n<p>Since both the pieces and textures are power of two, determining the pixel density of the object is relatively easy. Environments tend to look better with an even pixel density.</p>\n\n<p>Pay close attention to the behavior of your patterns and repeating details (mesh or textures) if you need to make smaller pieces than intended. If I needed a 128 cm corridor piece instead of 512, my wall pattern would be already divided up to support that, meaning that I can create shorter or longer pieces with an interval of 128 cm.</p>\n\n<p>If my pattern needed full 512 cm length, I’d be more locked by that design or have to find a way to cover broken up pattern.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/acb4998ce7c92b7e67dc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/acb4998ce7c92b7e67dc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"864\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p>The example set in this tutorial has a pixel density of 4 px/cm.</p></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/dfb1197d5a7706f0267a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/dfb1197d5a7706f0267a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"863\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>The system so far</h2>\n\n<p>These are the pieces we have gone through so far (except for the custom piece).</p>\n\n<p>The majority of this set is based of the 90<em>-</em>degree straight piece (with some added details for corners and stairs). You can get pretty far with a simple base if you need to.</p>\n\n<p>The 45-degree and 22.5-degree world pieces are quite a few stacked up, but it is good to remember that a lot of them are mirrored.</p>\n\n<p><em>Thank you for reading and happy creating!</em></p>";
export const images = ["assets/cookies-inline/1015599998ab3fdc778b.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/36d661a3f2af6d6e74c0.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/384e51382f6467ff91dc.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/3a7f4104c77698ceaaf5.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/80691dc305f63a462e00.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/82fc272fded1afb7fdf9.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/8c09bcd2a3fe98fc1edb.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/9efdbe0033c624ddf58e.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a31bdf6c95b52dc2fa72.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a4b721104f449919dc75.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/acb4998ce7c92b7e67dc.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/c436a3e8afe392e8f7eb.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/c93896a01eff5b6a351e.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/dd5090b4c70f1c65fcdd.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/dfb1197d5a7706f0267a.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/e58ccbcf11e54317e959.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/ee07287f4da0a2fc5f81.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/eee15e1bda15056d45fd.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/f18b71621411215fd5e1.webp"];
